**Read progressively:** [Termodinâmica dos Materiais — Cota, 2026](../sources/Termodinamica-dos-Materiais-Cota.lingua#r_05ES8F38ASNQEQMZ0HY6709Z0Z), the complete twelve-chapter book with exercises through this section; [Fundamentals of Materials Science — MIT 3.012](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Materials-Science-MIT-3-012.lingua#r_1JE4A3P3KKM0FZXERWFATXCS1Z), the thermodynamics sequence; [Phases, diffusion and solidification — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Phases-and-Diffusion-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_4HHCB32507KCQ5FPHGT4JD5GTB), the complete phase and diffusion packages. Read [Phase Transitions — Bhadeshia and Yan](../../../materials/metals-and-metallurgical-production/sources/Phase-Transitions-Bhadeshia-Yan.lingua#r_2PMPJ2B4GPGDT62S567TX1DCPJ) in full as an English introductory companion, including its worked examples. Begin with the shared chemical energy and entropy Records. Later, work the kinetics slides, summaries and available problems in [Materials kinetics — MIT 3.205 and 3.21](../sources/Kinetic-Processes-in-Materials-MIT.lingua#r_2BZVQ9NG9Y2XTF4QG0H3JYYS16). Their source Record explains missing figures and commercial textbook dependencies.

A structural description says what a material is like now. Equilibrium asks which states are favored under specified constraints; kinetics asks which changes occur on the available timescale. Heating, mixing or changing atmosphere can alter both. An observed persistence does not by itself establish thermodynamic stability.

**Work:** Choose a processing change for the recurring material. Separate energy/matter exchanges, possible equilibrium states, mechanisms, timescales and observations. Identify what the current structural account cannot predict. Read the thermodynamic foundations slowly before using a phase diagram or fitting a rate.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
**Continue in materials engineering:** [Choose a system and track heat, work, and enthalpy](../../../chemistry/general-chemistry/energy/Systems-heat-work-and-enthalpy.lingua#r_545JZM2734935DFWXBV90WM5PE).

